trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Vital signs are normal — no fever or tachycardia to suggest active inflammation or infection. Abdominal exam shows only mild diffuse discomfort without peritoneal signs, organomegaly, or masses — reassuring against malignancy, obstruction, or localized inflammatory disease. This further supports a functional or low-grade inflammatory process (e.g., IBS or microscopic colitis), but does not exclude them. Next, domain 8: most informative laboratory/imaging tests. Given chronic diarrhea with mucus and urgency but no alarm features, first-line testing should include:  
- Fecal calprotectin (to screen for neutrophilic intestinal inflammation — high in IBD, low in IBS);  
- Celiac serology (tTG-IgA + total IgA) — essential even without classic symptoms, as diarrhea-predominant celiac can be subtle;  
- Stool studies: ova & parasite (O&P) ×3 and Giardia antigen (given camping exposure), plus Clostridioides difficile toxin PCR (though no recent antibiotics, community-acquired cases occur).  

Of these, fecal calprotectin is the single most discriminating initial test to separate IBD from IBS — and is available in most clinical settings.

[TEST] Fecal calprotectin.

